<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jataka tales</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jataka tales are a large body of stories about the previous births of the Buddha, in which he appears as a human or an animal practising the virtues that lead to enlightenment. The Pali collection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> canon contains 547 tales, many set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> under kings named Brahmadatta. Carved on stupas such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchi">Sanchi</a> and retold across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, they are also a source on ancient Indian society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jataka-tales.jpg" alt="Jataka tales" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_India,_Andhra_Pradesh,_possibly_Amaravati,_Satavahana_period_-_Buddha%27s_Previous_Life_as_a_Woodpecker-_Javasakunda_Jataka_-_1970.350_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word jātaka means of the birth. According to the traditional account in the Pali Jātakanidāna, a prologue to the stories, Gautama vowed in front of a past Buddha, Dipankara, to become a Buddha himself, and then spent many lifetimes on the path. The stories from those lives are recorded as jātakas. The genre rests on the idea that the Buddha could recall all his past lives and used the memories to illustrate his teachings.</p><p>The tales may be very old. Depictions in early Indian art go back as far as the second century BCE, and many narratives survive in almost identical form in the canons of different schools, which suggests that they date from before the schisms between those schools. Sarah Shaw dates the verse portions of the Pali Jātaka to the fifth century BCE and says later parts were added up to the third century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In these stories the future Buddha may appear as a king, an outcaste, a deva or an animal, and whatever his form he shows a virtue that the tale teaches. Many follow a threefold plan: a narrative in the present, a story from a past life, and a link in which the characters of the past are identified with those of the present. The tales illustrate the perfections of a bodhisattva, such as generosity, and teach moral lessons within the framework of karma and rebirth.</p><p>The largest known collection is the Jātakatthavaṇṇanā of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> school, part of the Khuddaka Nikaya of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pali-canon">Pali Canon</a>, which holds 547 jātakas in verse and prose and was collected around 500 CE. Other sources include the Cariyāpiṭaka, which includes 35 jātakas, and Āryaśūra's Sanskrit Jātakamālā.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Jātakas were among the first texts translated into Chinese, and Kāng Sēnghuì, working at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> around 247, was one of their first translators. The earliest known illustrations are on the railings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a> stupa and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchi">Sanchi</a>, from the late second to the first century BCE, and they later appear at Ajanta and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagan">Bagan</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananda-temple">Ananda Temple</a> depicts 554 tales. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a> said that jātaka plays were performed throughout the five countries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 7th century.</p><p>The Vessantara Jataka is still recited and performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> countries. E. B. Cowell and others translated the Pali collection into English in six volumes between 1895 and 1907. A jātaka was also adapted by the 10th-century Shia scholar Ibn Bābūya as Balawhar wa-Būdāsf, which became the Christian story of Barlaam and Joasaph.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jataka tales” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They mostly depict <b>Jataka tales</b>, avadanas and legends of the Buddha, in the tradition of the Sarvāstivāda school.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The paintings predominantly narrate the <b>Jataka tales</b>, legends of the Buddha's previous births as an animal or a human.</blockquote><a href="/work/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>It shares many stories with the Buddhist <b>Jataka tales</b>, and Olivelle writes that it is uncertain whether its author borrowed from the Jatakas or the Mahabharata, or drew on a common treasury of oral and literary tales.</blockquote><a href="/work/panchatantra">Panchatantra · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Jataka tales</b> set many stories in the reigns of kings called Brahmadatta of Varanasi, and Jain tradition makes the 23rd tirthankara, Parshvanatha, the son of a Kashi king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kashi">Kingdom of Kashi</a></li></ul></section>
